# Line-ending policy for files whose SHA-256 is recorded somewhere.
#
# Why this file exists (batch 4, step 1). `experiments/align05_title_rules_v1.json`
# is a FROZEN artefact whose SHA-256 is cited in a pre-registration
# (`experiments/ALIGN05_PREREG.md`, amendment A2) and hard-checked by
# `experiments/build_align05_validation_sample.py`. On a clone with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows) Git rewrites its 55 LF newlines to
# CRLF in the working tree, so hashing the bytes on disk yields
#   9F0410507C23313C99517CEC9D40A1CC0697CD29192A89357F0E321234F1429D
# while hashing the LF-normalised bytes -- which is what the blob stores and what
# A2 records -- yields
#   09BC778E67C884ECE38EDEC81B57DF70123EBE331F932D9441D6A12DBFCB7E36
# Both are "correct" for the same logical file; only one is reproducible across
# machines. Forcing eol=lf makes the working tree byte-identical to the blob on
# every platform, so a single recorded hash verifies everywhere. See A4.
#
# Rule: any file whose content hash is (or may later be) recorded in a
# pre-registration, registry, metrics.json or checksums file MUST be listed here
# with eol=lf, and every hash check MUST compare LF-normalised bytes.

# --- files whose hashes are recorded today -----------------------------------
# experiments/align05_title_rules_v1.json : SHA-256 cited in ALIGN05_PREREG A2/A4
experiments/align05_title_rules_v1.json  eol=lf
# datasets/input_checksums.sha256 holds the 9 canonical input hashes; CRLF would
# also break `sha256sum -c` path parsing (trailing \r in the filename)
datasets/input_checksums.sha256          eol=lf

# --- categories that would carry a hash if one is ever recorded --------------
*.json       text eol=lf
*.sha256     text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.toml       text eol=lf
*.cfg        text eol=lf
*.ini        text eol=lf

# --- source and prose: normalise so future hashes are stable -----------------
*.py         text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.tex        text eol=lf
*.bib        text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf

# --- never normalise: binary content would be corrupted ----------------------
*.pdf        binary
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.jpeg       binary
*.gz         binary
*.zip        binary
*.pkl        binary
*.npy        binary
*.npz        binary
*.pt         binary
*.bin        binary
